§ `Hand-written cases: persons in the seat`

-- Case: Ahmed is under a prohibition order and keeps a hunting rifle at home (ss 117.01(1), 117.01(4))
#ASSERT `Under section 117.01, is possessing a weapon while prohibited by an order, or wilfully failing to surrender an authorization, licence or registration certificate, an offence on what I did` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- a firearm, while prohibited by an order: the offence is made out
#ASSERT `Under section 117.01, is possessing a weapon while prohibited by an order, or wilfully failing to surrender an authorization, licence or registration certificate, an offence on what I did` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING   -- the rifle is held under a licence issued as the result of an order under s 113(1): s 117.01(1) is out by s 117.01(4), but the composite is NOTHING, not FALSE, because no other limb says no (the next case asks s 117.01(4) itself)
#ASSERT `Under section 117.01(4), does section 117.01(1) not apply to me because I possessed a firearm in accordance with an authorization or licence issued as the result of an order under section 113(1)` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- the rifle is held in accordance with a licence issued as the result of an order under s 113(1): s 117.01(1) does not apply to him
#ASSERT `Under section 117.01, is possessing a weapon while prohibited by an order, or wilfully failing to surrender an authorization, licence or registration certificate, an offence on what I did` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING   -- not prohibited by any order: the section does not reach the case, and says nothing about it

-- Case: Bea pushes past a police officer who is arresting her friend (s 129(a))
#ASSERT `Under section 129, is resisting or obstructing an officer, or a person executing process, or failing to assist an officer, an offence on what I did` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- a peace officer in the execution of their duty, resisted
#ASSERT `Under section 129, is resisting or obstructing an officer, or a person executing process, or failing to assist an officer, an offence on what I did` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING   -- the person obstructed is not stated to be a public officer or peace officer: not shown

-- Case: Cal tells a witness, with threats, not to turn up at the trial (s 139(3)(a))
#ASSERT `Under section 139(3), am I deemed wilfully to have attempted to obstruct, pervert or defeat the course of justice` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- dissuading a person by threats from giving evidence is deemed wilful obstruction
#ASSERT `Under section 139(3), am I deemed wilfully to have attempted to obstruct, pervert or defeat the course of justice` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING   -- no judicial proceeding, existing or proposed: not within the deeming limb

-- Case: Dee swears an affidavit she knows is false (ss 131(1), 131(3), 134(1), 134(2))
#ASSERT `Under section 131(1), is making a false statement under oath or solemn affirmation, knowing it is false and with intent to mislead, perjury on what I did` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- perjury
#ASSERT `Under section 131(1), is making a false statement under oath or solemn affirmation, knowing it is false and with intent to mislead, perjury on what I did` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE   -- she was not specially permitted, authorized or required by law to make it: s 131(3) takes the case out of perjury
#ASSERT `Under section 134(1), is making a false statement under oath or solemn affirmation, without being permitted, authorized or required by law to make it, an offence on what I did` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- the same facts fall within the summary offence of s 134(1)
#ASSERT `Under section 134(1), is making a false statement under oath or solemn affirmation, without being permitted, authorized or required by law to make it, an offence on what I did` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE   -- made in the course of a criminal investigation: s 134(2) says (1) does not apply

-- Case: Eli misses court while on a release order (ss 145(2)(a), 145(8))
#ASSERT `Under section 145(2)(a), is failing without lawful excuse to attend court as a release order requires an offence on what I did` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- at large on a release order, failed to attend, no lawful excuse
#ASSERT `Under section 145(2)(a), is failing without lawful excuse to attend court as a release order requires an offence on what I did` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE   -- the Attorney General had elected under section 50 of the Contraventions Act before the failure: a lawful excuse by s 145(8)
#ASSERT `Under section 145(2)(a), is failing without lawful excuse to attend court as a release order requires an offence on what I did` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING   -- a lawful excuse of the person's own: the paragraph is limited by "without lawful excuse"

-- Case: Fay ignores a summons because the summons describes the offence badly (ss 145(3), 145(6))
#ASSERT `Under section 145(3), is failing without lawful excuse to appear or attend court as an appearance notice or summons requires an offence on what I did` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- s 145(6): a defective statement of the alleged offence is not a lawful excuse
#ASSERT `Under section 145(3), is failing without lawful excuse to appear or attend court as an appearance notice or summons requires an offence on what I did` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING   -- a lawful excuse on other grounds: not shown to be an offence

-- Case: Gus has a hearing on an application for a prohibition order (ss 111(5), 111(5) period)
#ASSERT `Under section 111(5), is the provincial court judge bound to make an order prohibiting me from possessing firearms and similar things` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- concluded, and the judge is satisfied the circumstances exist
#ASSERT `Under section 111(5), is the provincial court judge bound to make an order prohibiting me from possessing firearms and similar things` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE   -- the judge is not satisfied: "If ... satisfied" is not met

-- Case: Hal gave evidence in one trial and later gave contrary evidence in another (s 136(1))
#ASSERT `Under section 136(1), can I be convicted of giving evidence contrary to my previous evidence as a witness in a judicial proceeding` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- the court is satisfied beyond a reasonable doubt that he intended to mislead
#ASSERT `Under section 136(1), can I be convicted of giving evidence contrary to my previous evidence as a witness in a judicial proceeding` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE   -- no conviction "unless the court ... is satisfied beyond a reasonable doubt" of intent to mislead
#ASSERT `Under section 136(1), can I be convicted of giving evidence contrary to my previous evidence as a witness in a judicial proceeding` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING   -- the earlier evidence is not material (not "evidence" for s 136 by s 136(2) ... as the person states it): the offence is not shown

-- Case: Ida has broken a federal statute that carries its own penalty (s 126(1))
#ASSERT `Under section 126(1), is intentionally contravening an Act of Parliament, for which no punishment is expressly provided, an offence on what I did` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- intentional contravention of an Act of Parliament, no excuse, no punishment provided elsewhere
#ASSERT `Under section 126(1), is intentionally contravening an Act of Parliament, for which no punishment is expressly provided, an offence on what I did` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE   -- a punishment is expressly provided by law: "unless a punishment is expressly provided by law"

-- Case: Jo, an official, takes a loan from a contractor in return for help with a government contract (s 121(1)(a))
#ASSERT `Under section 121(1), is giving, taking or arranging a benefit in dealings with the government an offence on what I did` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- whether or not Jo can in fact deliver the help
#ASSERT `Under section 121(1), is giving, taking or arranging a benefit in dealings with the government an offence on what I did` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING   -- a contractor who paid an official, with the written consent of the head of the branch: paragraph (b) says no ("unless the person has the consent in writing"), but the composite is NOTHING because the text says no only to that paragraph

-- Case: Kim is a police officer who carries a prohibited weapon on duty (ss 117.07, 117.1)
#ASSERT `Under section 117.07, am I, as a public officer, exempt from guilt of an offence under the Criminal Code or the Firearms Act by reason only of what I did in my duties` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- a public officer, in the course of duties, by reason only of possessing: exempt
#ASSERT `Under section 117.07, am I, as a public officer, exempt from guilt of an offence under the Criminal Code or the Firearms Act by reason only of what I did in my duties` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE   -- subject to a prohibition order and acting contrary to it: s 117.1 says the exemption does not apply

-- ====================================================================================================
-- cases-seat questions from part ccc6 (merged by the lead on 2026-11-12; unchanged text follows)
-- ====================================================================================================
-- ccc6-cases-seat.l4 -- cases for the seat layer: for each question, the facts a real accused would state and the answer the text gives (decided from the text, written before the run).
-- Where a question's answer can be "not covered" (NOTHING) or "no" (JUST FALSE), a case asserts that too. A case for a group is marked with the facts of a real person.


§ `s 150 -- the words of this Part`

#ASSERT `Under section 150, am I a guardian of the other person` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- in law only
#ASSERT `Under section 150, am I a guardian of the other person` FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING   -- neither: "includes" leaves the list open

#ASSERT `Under section 150, is the place a public place` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- as of right
#ASSERT `Under section 150, is the place a public place` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING   -- a private place

#ASSERT `Under section 150, is the place a theatre` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- open and entertainments
#ASSERT `Under section 150, is the place a theatre` TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING   -- no entertainments: not covered

#ASSERT `Under section 150, is the part of the body in question a sexual organ` `s 150 the anal region` EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `Under section 150, is the part of the body in question a sexual organ` `s 150 a part of the body that is none of those` EQUALS NOTHING   -- the list is open

§ `s 150.1 -- consent as a defence, and mistake of age`

#ASSERT `Under section 150.1, what does the section say about whether the complainant's consent is a defence` 151 "" 13 40 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 150.1 consent is not a defence, by subsection (1)`   -- a 40-year-old and a 13-year-old
#ASSERT `Under section 150.1, what does the section say about whether the complainant's consent is a defence` 151 "" 13 14 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 150.1 consent is a defence, by subsection (2), (2.1), (2.2) or (2.3)`   -- a 14-year-old and a 13-year-old: less than two years apart
#ASSERT `Under section 150.1, what does the section say about whether the complainant's consent is a defence` 280 "" 15 25 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 150.1 section 150.1 does not say whether consent is a defence`   -- an offence the section does not name
#ASSERT `Under section 150.1, what does the section say about whether the complainant's consent is a defence` 151 "" 15 25 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 150.1 subsection (1) says consent is not a defence and subsection (2.3) says it is, and the text does not say which prevails`   -- married immediately before: (1) and (2.3) both apply (finding)

#ASSERT `Under section 150.1(3), does the bar apply so that I may not be tried because I was aged twelve or thirteen` 151 "" 12 FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE   -- twelve, in none of the three relationships: the bar applies
