-- An answer of NOTHING asserted here is the "not covered" case: the text does not say.

§ `Is the place a common gaming house or a common betting house (section 197)`

-- a bank is kept by one but not all of the players, in a place kept or used to play games: 197(1) common gaming house (b)(i)
#ASSERT `Under section 197(1), is the place I am accused of keeping a common gaming house` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- the proceeds go to the keeper and the place is not a club: (b)(ii), no exception
#ASSERT `Under section 197(1), is the place I am accused of keeping a common gaming house` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- the same, but a genuine social club whose games pay the keeper nothing and charge no fee: the 197(2) exception applies
#ASSERT `Under section 197(1), is the place I am accused of keeping a common gaming house` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- not a place: "common gaming house means a place that is ..."
#ASSERT `Under section 197(1), is the place I am accused of keeping a common gaming house` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE

-- opened, kept or used so that people can bet: 197(1) common betting house (a)
#ASSERT `Under section 197(1), is the place I am accused of keeping a common betting house` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- used to receive, record or pay bets: (b)
#ASSERT `Under section 197(1), is the place I am accused of keeping a common betting house` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- a place, but neither use: "common betting house means a place that is opened, kept or used for the purpose of ..."
#ASSERT `Under section 197(1), is the place I am accused of keeping a common betting house` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE

-- "keeper includes a person who (a) is an owner or occupier of a place"
#ASSERT `Under section 197(1), am I the keeper of the place` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- (e) uses a place permanently or temporarily
#ASSERT `Under section 197(1), am I the keeper of the place` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- "keeper includes ...": the list is not closed, so a person in none of (a) to (e) is not covered, not excluded
#ASSERT `Under section 197(1), am I the keeper of the place` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING

-- "the onus of proving that a place is not a common gaming house ... is on the accused"
#ASSERT `Under section 197(3), is it for me, not the prosecution, to prove that the place falls within the exception in section 197(2)` EQUALS TRUE

-- "a place may be a common gaming house although part of the game is played elsewhere": that circumstance does not prevent it
-- RECONCILED 2026-11-12: was `Under section 197(4), does it prevent the place from being a common gaming house that part of the game is played elsewhere, or that it was used only once` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE; direction: the old title asked whether the circumstance PREVENTS the place from being a common gaming house, but the function answers `the circumstance does not prevent`, so JUST TRUE read as "yes, it prevents"; the new title asks the function's own question
#ASSERT `Under section 197(4), can the place still be a common gaming house although part of the game is played elsewhere, or it was used only once` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- used on only one occasion and no other place used another time: the text does not say it prevents or does not prevent, it says only when it does not prevent
-- RECONCILED 2026-11-12: was `Under section 197(4), does it prevent the place from being a common gaming house that part of the game is played elsewhere, or that it was used only once` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING; direction: the old title asked whether the circumstance PREVENTS the place from being a common gaming house, but the function answers `the circumstance does not prevent`, so JUST TRUE read as "yes, it prevents"; the new title asks the function's own question
#ASSERT `Under section 197(4), can the place still be a common gaming house although part of the game is played elsewhere, or it was used only once` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- only one occasion here, but another place was used on another occasion in that way: the circumstance does not prevent
-- RECONCILED 2026-11-12: was `Under section 197(4), does it prevent the place from being a common gaming house that part of the game is played elsewhere, or that it was used only once` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE; direction: the old title asked whether the circumstance PREVENTS the place from being a common gaming house, but the function answers `the circumstance does not prevent`, so JUST TRUE read as "yes, it prevents"; the new title asks the function's own question
#ASSERT `Under section 197(4), can the place still be a common gaming house although part of the game is played elsewhere, or it was used only once` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE

